    "slug": "student-counselling",
    "title": "Student Counselling",
    "subtitle": "Confidence, motivation, clarity and preparedness to move forward.",
    "image": "/images/services/Student Counselling.png",
    "content": "STUDENT COUNSELLING**\n\n**Helping students move forward with confidence, clarity and the right direction.**\n\nStudent life is an important phase of growth. Along with studies, students have to deal with academic pressure, expectations from parents, examinations, peer influence, social media, setbacks, changing environments and many other challenges.\n\nSometimes a student has the ability and potential to do well, but is unable to use it effectively. At other times, the pressure around the student becomes so high that confidence, motivation or focus starts getting affected.\n\n**Social Counselling provides a structured and supportive conversation to understand what is actually holding the student back and help them move forward with greater confidence, clarity and responsibility.**\n\nStudent Counselling is **not academic counselling or career counselling**. It focuses on the social, behavioural, emotional and adjustment-related aspects that can affect a student's ability to deal with their education and student life effectively.\n\n\n\n\n\n**What may bring a student to Social Counselling?**\n\nEvery student is different. Some may approach because of a specific concern, while others may simply need someone to help them understand and handle a difficult situation.\n\nCommon areas may include:\n\n· Excessive influence of **digital and social media**;\n\n· **Examination anxiety and fear of failure**;\n\n· Difficulty handling **academic setbacks, rejection or disappointment**;\n\n· **Peer influence, comparison and pressure**;\n\n· Difficulty handling **academic pressure**;\n\n· Pressure or high expectations from parents;\n\n· Difficulty managing **studies, time and priorities**;\n\n· Loss of motivation or declining interest in studies;\n\n· Difficulty maintaining **discipline and consistency**;\n\n· Difficulty handling feedback, criticism or expectations;\n\n· Balancing studies with hobbies, interests and other activities;\n\n· Communication difficulties with parents around studies;\n\n· Difficulty adapting to college or university life;\n\n· Difficulty adjusting to a new city, institution or environment;\n\n· Transition from school to higher education; and\n\n· Difficulty developing confidence and taking greater responsibility for their own progress.\n\nSometimes the concern may not be obvious even to the student.\n\n**The first step is therefore to understand what is really happening rather than simply reacting to the visible problem.**\n\n\n\n\n\n**How Social Counselling can help**\n\nSocial Counselling does not automatically assume that the student is responsible for the problem. Nor does it automatically assume that the parents are putting unnecessary pressure on the student.\n\nThe Counsellor seeks to understand **both sides** and determine what may actually be contributing to the situation.\n\nThe conversation may therefore involve the student, the parents, or both.\n\n**Understand the Situation**\n\nThe Counsellor seeks to understand:\n\n· what the student is experiencing;\n\n· what the parents expect;\n\n· what the student expects from themselves;\n\n· what difficulties are actually being faced; and\n\n· what may be influencing the student's behaviour, confidence or motivation.\n\n**Understand Before Advising**\n\nThe Counsellor does not simply accept the version presented by either the student or the parent.\n\nAppropriate and gentle questions may be asked to understand the situation better and identify where the actual difficulty lies.\n\n**Set Realistic Expectations**\n\nParents may sometimes have expectations that need to be better understood or recalibrated.\n\nSimilarly, a student may have expectations, fears or assumptions that need to be examined.\n\nSocial Counselling helps bring both sides towards **realistic and clearly understood expectations**.\n\n**Build Confidence**\n\nA student may have the ability and potential but lack the confidence to use it.\n\nThe Counsellor helps the student identify what is holding them back and encourages them to look at their own capabilities more positively.\n\nThe underlying approach is simple:\n\n**“Why not? What is stopping you?”**\n\nThe objective is not to give empty motivation, but to build confidence around **realistic expectations, effort, planning and responsibility**.\n\n**Handle Pressure, Setbacks and Failure**\n\nExaminations, academic setbacks, criticism or comparison with others can sometimes have a disproportionate impact on a student's confidence.\n\nThe Counsellor helps the student look at these situations more consciously, understand what can be learnt from them and prepare to move forward.\n\n**Develop Focus and Discipline**\n\nWhere required, the conversation may help the student look at:\n\n· how they are using their time;\n\n· what is distracting them;\n\n· whether their priorities are clear;\n\n· how consistently they are working; and\n\n· what changes in routine or approach may help.\n\n**Help Navigate Peer & Social Influence**\n\nStudents are increasingly exposed to comparisons, social-media content and opinions of their peers.\n\nSocial Counselling can help them understand the difference between **what others are doing, what others are saying and what is actually appropriate for them**.\n\n**Encourage Responsibility**\n\nThe objective is not to make the student dependent on parents, teachers or the Counsellor for every decision.\n\nThe student should gradually develop the confidence to understand their responsibilities, make conscious choices and take ownership of their own progress.\n\n\n\n\n\n**What Social Counselling does NOT cover**\n\nStudent Counselling is focused on the **social and behavioural aspects of student life**.\n\nIt does not undertake:\n\n· academic counselling;\n\n· selection of subjects, streams or courses;\n\n· career counselling;\n\n· selection of colleges or universities;\n\n· assessment of career suitability;\n\n· detailed academic planning or tutoring; or\n\n· specialist psychological or clinical intervention.\n\nWhere a concern falls outside the scope of Social Counselling, the individual may be advised to seek appropriate professional or specialist assistance.\n\n\n\n\n\n**What should a student expect from the conversation?**\n\nThe student should expect a conversation where:\n\n· their perspective will be heard;\n\n· they will be encouraged to speak openly;\n\n· their concerns will be understood before conclusions are drawn;\n\n· parents' expectations may also be examined where relevant;\n\n· difficult questions may be asked respectfully;\n\n· unrealistic expectations may be challenged;\n\n· their strengths and potential will be recognised;\n\n· they will be encouraged to think positively and constructively; and\n\n· the conversation will focus on **what can realistically be done next**.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n**Target Outcome\\***\n\n**Confidence, Planning & Clarity** — The student should feel confident to take the next steps in the right direction, with greater clarity, appropriate planning and confidence in their ability to move forward.\n\n**Motivation & Encouragement — The student should emerge as a more motivated individual, having received the requisite encouragement and support to work towards the expectations mutually understood and agreed with the parents.**\n\n**\\*Attention is drawn to the Client T&Cs and it is expressly communicated that Social Counselling cannot guarantee any particular outcome post counselling due to multiple external factors not within the control of Social Counselling. Please refer to the Client T&Cs for more details. Target outcome gets fine-tuned for each individual case.**"
